  "body": "cc/ @yutakahirano\n\nI investigated how continuous call to `close()` and `abort()` of WritableStream results in `[[onClose]]` and `[[onAbort]]` call.\n\nIf `close()` returns a non-promise value synchronously, i.e. it complete successfully and synchronously, the returned value will be resolved to a promise, and \"upon fulfillment\" code will be run asynchronously. This means the state doesn't change to `\"closed\"` immediately on `close()` call. It stays to be `\"closing\"`. If `abort()` is called right after `closed()` call in the same task, it invokes `[[onAbort]]` though `close()` has finished synchronously.\n\nI wonder if we want to allow `[[onAbort]]` being called after successful and synchronous `[[onClose]]`. Maybe we want to hide this kind of cumbersome state checking inside streams not to bother underlying sink implementer. Like what we're doing for `write()`, we could pass some method like `signalDone` to have a stream get notified of completion of a delegate method synchronously and change the state synchronously.\n\nThis question can be generalized as to figure out \"clear rule to choose notification method for delegate functions on underlying sink/source\".\n\nI briefly discussed this with @yutakahirano. We also came up with an alternative that we delay both `[[onClose]]` and `[[onAbort]]` execution by putting them into the micro task queue by having an internal promise for this purpose and append (then-ing) state changing code, `[[onClose]]` and `[[onAbort]]` to the promise.\n\n```\nthis.current = this.current.then(\n  => check_state(); call_delegate().then(\n    => change_state(); ...) ...\n```\n",
